## Asking before you commit

**How much is it?**
{LANGUAGE-text}
*{pronunciation-guide-in-italics}*

**Please turn on the meter.**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

Say this the instant you get into any taxi. If the driver refuses, get out.

**Can I see the menu, please?**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

{OPTIONAL — language-appropriate additional questions:
- "Is this the terrace price or the indoor price?" (Mediterranean)
- "Is this the day tariff?" (Turkey — meter rate gets doubled at night)
- "What is the price per gram/jin/tola?" (weight-priced items)
- "Do you accept foreign credit cards?" (cash-heavy countries)
- "Is there a service fee?" (tea-house / hotpot / restaurant cover-charge scams)}

## Declining and leaving

**No, thank you.**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

The single most useful phrase in this appendix. Firm, polite, understood everywhere. Walking away is not rude.

**I'm not interested.**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

**Please leave me alone.**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

**Don't touch me.**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

## Responding to a scam in progress

**I'm calling the police.**
{text}
*{pronunciation}*

This is the firmest card in the deck. **{EMERGENCY-NUMBER}** is {country's police emergency line}. Say the phrase, hold up your phone, and dial.

**I'll call my bank.**
{text}

**I was scammed. Please help.**
{text}

The verb *{local-verb-for-scammed}* is the specific {LANGUAGE} term — communicates the situation with more urgency than any English translation.

**Please give me an itemized receipt.**
{text}

**I didn't order this.**
{text}

**This is not what we agreed.**
{text}

